  {"cause":"Upstream dependency refusing connections at startup causing fatal abort","detail":"exit_code=134/SIGABRT typically indicates the process called abort() — in many runtimes this happens when a required upstream (e.g. auth service, config endpoint, broker) is unreachable at initialization and the application treats it as unrecoverable. The crashloop with count=12 back-offs confirms repeated startup failures rather than a mid-run instability, consistent with a dependency that is consistently rejecting or timing out TCP/TLS handshakes on each restart attempt.","anchored_facts":["process_crash","crashloop"],"confidence":0.55},
  {"cause":"TLS certificate validation failure against an upstream endpoint triggering abort()","detail":"SIGABRT at exit_code=134 without OOM or scheduling issues points to a software-initiated abort. If bot-service validates a peer TLS certificate on startup (e.g. against an internal CA, a secrets vault, or an mTLS-gated API) and the cert is expired, rotated, or the CA bundle is stale in this preprod environment, strict TLS libraries call abort() on fatal handshake errors. The consistent crash-loop cadence supports a deterministic failure path rather than transient flapping.","anchored_facts":["process_crash","crashloop"],"confidence":0.40},
  {"cause":"DNS resolution failure for a required upstream preventing process initialization","detail":"If the service resolves upstream hostnames at startup (common for queue brokers, service-mesh control planes, or external APIs) and DNS returns NXDOMAIN or times out — possible in preprod-kingdom1 if the namespace network policy or CoreDNS config diverges from prod — some SDKs escalate to abort() when a critical host is unresolvable. Repeated BackOff events (count=12) with no evidence of resource pressure or scheduling issues narrows the failure to within the container startup sequence, consistent with a DNS/network dependency path.","anchored_facts":["process_crash","crashloop"],"confidence":0.35}
]}

{"hypotheses":[
  {"cause":"Unrecovered application panic (Go runtime or equivalent) causing SIGABRT on every startup", "detail":"Exit code 134 maps to signal 6 (SIGABRT), which is the canonical signature of an unrecovered Go goroutine panic — the runtime calls abort() after printing the stack trace. The crash is deterministic (max_restart_count=0 yet BackOff count=12), meaning the same code path fails on every container start. Likely candidates: nil pointer dereference, out-of-bounds slice access, or a failed type assertion in initialization code that runs before any recovery middleware is installed.", "anchored_facts":["process_crash","crashloop"], "confidence":0.72},
  {"cause":"Fatal startup assertion or mandatory config/env-var validation calling abort()", "detail":"Some frameworks and internal SDKs call abort() (or log.Fatal → os.Exit(2) wrapped in a signal handler that re-raises SIGABRT) when a required configuration key is missing or fails validation at boot. The deterministic crash-loop pattern with BackOff count=12 and no variation in exit code strongly suggests the failure occurs before the service reaches a steady state — consistent with early-lifecycle initialization code that asserts required values are present.", "anchored_facts":["process_crash","crashloop"], "confidence":0.55},
  {"cause":"Heap corruption or CGO/native-library assertion triggering SIGABRT from the C allocator", "detail":"If bot-service links native code via CGO (e.g., a compression, crypto, or serialization library), a corrupted heap or a failed C-level assert() will raise SIGABRT, producing exit_code=134. Unlike OOM (which is excluded), this class of crash is silent in Kubernetes terminated-state logs ('text matches suppressed' in the oom_killed fact confirms log suppression is occurring) and repeats identically on every restart because the corrupting input or state is reproduced from a persistent source (message queue, config mount, or environment variable).", "anchored_facts":["process_crash","crashloop"], "confidence":0.32}
]}

{"hypotheses":[
  {"cause":"Native heap corruption triggering allocator abort()","detail":"exit_code=134/SIGABRT is the canonical outcome when glibc malloc (or jemalloc/tcmalloc) detects heap metadata corruption — double-free, use-after-free, or buffer overrun — and self-signals SIGABRT. In a bot-service context this is typical of P/Invoke or JNI interop where unmanaged code corrupts the managed heap boundary, or of a native dependency with a memory-safety bug. The process is terminating itself deliberately rather than being killed externally, which is why oom_killed is ✗ despite exit_code=134.","anchored_facts":["process_crash","crashloop"],"confidence":0.72},
  {"cause":"CLR/JVM unrecoverable runtime fault (ExecutionEngineException / HotSpot fatal error)","detail":"The .NET CLR raises SIGABRT on fatal internal errors: ExecutionEngineException, corrupted object headers, GC heap invariant violations, or stack overflow in a native frame where the CLR cannot unwind safely. The JVM similarly calls abort() on HotSpot internal assertions or JNI frame corruption. Both runtimes produce exit_code=134 with no OOM marker — matching the ✗ oom_killed / ✓ process_crash pattern exactly. The repeating crash-loop (BackOff count=12) suggests the fault is deterministic and reproducible rather than transient resource exhaustion.","anchored_facts":["process_crash","crashloop"],"confidence":0.65},
  {"cause":"Unhandled exception in async/background thread calling std::terminate or runtime abort handler","detail":"In Go (cgo), .NET (unobserved Task exception on older runtimes), or C++ (std::terminate from a noexcept-violated async callback), an exception that escapes a background goroutine/thread boundary can invoke the runtime's abort handler, producing SIGABRT. This is consistent with a service that handles concurrent bot events: a panic in a goroutine without a recover(), or a finalizer thread crash in .NET, will bring down the whole process with signal 6. The low max_restart_count=0 at pod level alongside BackOff count=12 at namespace event level suggests the container exits quickly and consistently each restart cycle.","anchored_facts":["process_crash","crashloop"],"confidence":0.55}
]}

